WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
Start with seven units and add three more.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.200
Regrouping them into two groups of five makes the total easier to check.

3
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.550
Ten marks still represent ten units; organization changes, not quantity.

4
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:29.600
In one hundred five, one occupies the hundreds column and five the units column.

5
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.450
Zero indicates that the tens column is empty.

6
00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:43.800
Removing that zero gives fifteen; moving a digit changes its place value.

7
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:51.150
The equation x plus three equals eight contains an unknown quantity.

8
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:58.000
Subtract three from both sides, not just one.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.350
This leaves x equals five; the symbol lets us operate before knowing its value.

10
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:12.700
Adding one, two, three, four, five and six gives twenty-one.

11
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:19.550
Sigma notation expresses the same sum with an index and two limits.

12
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:26.900
The notation compresses the description; it does not automatically perform the computation.

13
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:34.250
Assume every element of A also belongs to B.

14
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:41.100
If x belongs to A, those two premises imply that x belongs to B.

15
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:49.517
Deduction preserves the logical relation; it does not itself prove the premises describe the world.

16
00:01:50.017 --> 00:01:57.567
For y equals x squared, compare x equals one with one plus h.

17
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:04.417
The average slope is two plus h; as h shrinks it approaches two.

18
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:11.767
The limit describes local change, not an average over a large interval.

